"A wanderer named Vaccha asked where an awakened person is reborn after death. Theyre reborn doesnt apply, Vaccha. Then are they not reborn? Theyre not reborn doesnt apply. Both? Neither? Each time the answer was that it does not apply, and Vaccha said that he had lost what clarity he had. The Buddha then asked him a question. If a fire were burning in front of you, you would know it, and you would know that it burned in dependence on grass and logs. If it went out, you would know that too. But suppose someone asked: This fire in front of you that is quenched: in what direction did it go—east, south, west, or north? Vaccha said: It doesnt apply, worthy Gotama. The fire depended on grass and logs as fuel. In the same way, the Buddha said, everything by which an awakened person might be described has been given up, and none of these apply.": "4d3866f94257",
"Aristotle called one principle the most certain of all: the same attribute cannot at the same time belong and not belong to the same subject and in the same respect. He added a second: there cannot be an intermediate between contradictories, but of one subject we must either affirm or deny any one predicate. The first rules out the third corner, both. The second rules out the fourth, neither. Anyone who denies the first principle, he said, cannot be argued with, since to say anything definite is already to rely on it, and a person who says nothing is from the start no better than a vegetable. Western logic was built on these two laws. Aristotles own wording leaves a door open, though. The attribute must belong and not belong in the same respect. The classical Indian reading of the third corner is that the thing is so in one respect and not so in another, which he would allow.": "cb8b6a5d704c",
"For the Buddhas own use of the four corners the closest Western parallel is Kant. Both face questions that seem to demand one of a fixed set of answers, both decline every answer, and both justify the refusal by exposing an assumption inside the question. The fire that went nowhere and the world that is neither finite nor infinite are solved in the same way. The Pyrrhonist skeptics of the last lesson also rejected four alternatives, and an ancient report gives Pyrrhos formula as: no more is than is not, or both is and is not, or neither is nor is not. They resemble Sañjaya more than the Buddha, since they give no account of why the answers fail.": "21afc1403ac9",
"If a fire goes out, which direction did it go? The question has no right answer because fires do not go anywhere.": "92541ec2caa8",
"In the twentieth century logicians built consistent formal systems in which a statement may be true, false, both, or neither. One of them was devised in 1977 for computers that receive conflicting information from different sources and must go on reasoning without concluding everything. Its four values are the four corners. The philosopher Graham Priest argues further that some contradictions are true, the liar sentence this sentence is false being the standard case, and with Jay Garfield he has read Nāgārjuna as holding that statements about the ultimate nature of things are of this kind. To say that things have no ultimate nature is to state their ultimate nature. This reading shows that the four corners are not illogical, since they have a rigorous logic of their own. Whether it is what Nāgārjuna meant is disputed. Many scholars hold that he denies all four corners precisely to avoid contradiction, and that his own statements are conventional truths.": "ceed83646629",
"Indian philosophers before the Buddha had a standard way of setting out the possible answers to a question. A thing is so, or it is not so, or it is both, or it is neither. One teacher of the day, Sañjaya, was known for refusing all four. I dont say its like this. I dont say its like that. I dont say its otherwise. I dont say its not so. And I dont deny its not so. The discourses call such teachers eel-wrigglers, and the Buddha did not admire them. His own use of the four alternatives is different, and one conversation shows how.": "2413facdacc0",
"Kant examined four old questions of metaphysics, among them whether the world had a beginning in time and whether it is finite in space. For each he set out a proof that it is so and an equally good proof that it is not, and called the result an antinomy. Reason, working correctly, contradicts itself. His solution was that both sides share a false assumption, that the world as a whole is an object that can be given in experience, with a definite size and age. Drop the assumption, and thesis and antithesis are both false. As it happens, whether the world is eternal and whether it is infinite are among the questions the Buddha declined to answer. Kants diagnosis has the same form as the simile of the fire. A question can be rejected without ignorance and without evasion, when one can say what is wrong with it.": "9c3cd98ba2c4",
"Kant found the same thing: asked whether the world is finite or infinite, he said both answers are false.": "152d3f206d15",
"Nāgārjuna drew the philosophical consequence. In the chapter on the Buddha he says that emptiness should not be asserted, nor non-emptiness, nor both, nor neither, and that these are spoken of only as designations. The commentary adds that the terms are used to counter their opposites. A statement in his hands is a tool against a particular error and is to be put down when the error is gone. That includes the statement that all things are empty, as the last lesson showed. Chan Buddhism made a training method of this. A teachers question to a student is often built so that every answer the student can think of is wrong, and the purpose is that the student stop looking for a place to stand. The method can be abused, and it has produced a good deal of nonsense. Its origin is the conversation with Vaccha, in which every answer was declined and a reason was given.": "cda3392979aa",
"Some questions carry a mistake inside them. Answering yes or no accepts the mistake.": "4e8146fe122d",
"The Buddha was asked four versions of one question and said of each, It doesnt apply. Then he explained why.": "a902a7b2c3aa",
"The Buddhas concern was practical. Holding tightly to any of the four answers keeps a person arguing and does not free anyone.": "6cad673d5d97",
"The Sanskrit word for a corner, koṭi, means an extreme or an end point. The four corners are the four places where a thinker can come to rest on a question, and the Buddhist use of them treats positions as things people occupy and defend, as well as things that are true or false. When Vaccha asked whether the Buddha held any conviction at all, the answer was that he had done away with convictions, because he had seen how form, feeling, perception, choices, and consciousness arise and pass away. Seeing has taken the place of holding a view. This is the step with no close Western parallel. Kant, Aristotle, and Priest all ask which statements are true. The Buddha asks that too, and then asks what the mind is doing when it grips one of them.": "d048e1eb1839",
"The likeness to Kant ends at what the refusal is for. Kant wanted to fix the boundary of what human beings can know, so that science could proceed securely inside it and faith have room outside. The Buddha gave another reason, and gave it to Vaccha in the same conversation. Each of the positions, he said, is the thicket of views, the desert of views, the twist of views, and they dont lead to disillusionment, dispassion, cessation, peace, insight, awakening, and extinguishment. To adopt any of the four is to take up a position about a self, and the taking up is the trouble. Kant leaves his reader with a limit. The Buddha leaves Vaccha with something to do, and the discourse ends with Vaccha saying that the teaching is like a great tree from which the bark and branches have fallen away, leaving only the heartwood.": "da14a7e95d6c",
"The simile shows what kind of refusal this is. The question about the fire has four possible answers and all are wrong, because the question assumes that a fire is a thing that goes somewhere. The four questions about the awakened person assume a self that either continues or stops. Deny the assumption and none of the answers can be given. Nāgārjuna later used the four corners in both directions. Sometimes he denies all four, as in the first verse of his main work: things do not arise from themselves, from another, from both, or without a cause. Sometimes he lists all four as teachings: Everything is real, and not real, both real and not real, neither real nor not real. This is the teaching of the Buddhas. The commentators explain the second as a sequence suited to listeners at different stages. They do not take it as four truths asserted at once.": "1b19a16986ed",
"This is careful reasoning. It is the opposite of saying that anything goes.": "c190cf0e45d1",
"Western listeners meet the four corners in two unhelpful forms. Some have been told that Eastern thought goes beyond logic, and they are ready to admire a contradiction. Others have had some training in logic and conclude that Buddhists cannot think straight. Both can be answered with an example from ordinary life. Ask whether the number seven is green. It is not green, and it is not some other color. It is not both, and neither green nor not green sounds wrong too. No one concludes that logic has failed. The question was badly formed, since numbers are not the kind of thing that has a color. Then tell the story of the fire. The Buddhas refusals come with a reason, and the reason is one a logician accepts.": "f7999d9f685c"
}
